 "subject": "STAN syntax - fixing parameters",
 "messages_listed": 3,
 "last_activity": "2013-09-01T18:17:52Z",
 "messages": [
  {
   "id": "lWHLkmTUumAJ",
   "from": "Dominic Edwards",
   "author": "fc51f0efa2fed18a",
   "date": "2013-09-01T13:45:29Z",
   "subject": "STAN syntax - fixing parameters",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin:0in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.0pt\">Hi,</p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin:0in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.0pt\"> </p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin:0in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.0pt\">I&#39;m currently\r\nbuilding a model to explain count data and am having a little difficulty with\r\nthe STAN syntax.  I&#39;ve attached the model, <i>ToyModel.stan</i>, and any help would be most appreciated. </p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin:0in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.0pt\"> </p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin:0in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.0pt\">1: How can I fix one\r\nelement from a vector of parameter? </p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin:0in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.0pt\"> </p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin:0in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.0pt\">Initially I tried (attached as <i>ToyModel1.stan</i>) :</p>\r\n\r\n<div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">transformed parameters</span><span style=\"color:#660\">{</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#660\">...</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>vector</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">NT</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#606\">Beta</span><span style=\"color:#660\">;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> <br></span><span style=\"color:#606\">Beta</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">0</span><span style=\"color:#660\">;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> <br></span><span style=\"color:#660\">...</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#660\">}</span></div></code></div><p style=\"margin:0in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.0pt\"><br></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin:0in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.0pt\"> </p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin:0in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.0pt\">However this leads\r\nto the following error:</p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin:0in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.0pt\">Error : Error in\r\nfunction stan::prob::normal_log(N4stan5agrad3varE): Random variable[1]  is 1.#QNAN:0, but must not be nan!</p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin:0in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.0pt\">error occurred\r\nduring calling the sampler; sampling not done</p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin:0in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.0pt\"> </p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin:0in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.0pt\">My work around for\r\nthis was to specify a strong prior on the first element of the Beta vector\r\nwhich &quot;works&quot; but isn&#39;t an ideal solution, it also leads to another question about STAN&#39;s syntax.</p><p style=\"margin:0in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.0pt\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\"> </span><br></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin:0in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.0pt\">Is it possible to\r\nput a prior on a slice of a vector? </p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin:0in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.0pt\">So in this case it\r\nwould be something like:</p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin:0in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.0pt\"> </p>\r\n\r\n<div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">model</span><span style=\"color:#660\">{</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#660\">...</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#606\">Beta</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#066\">2</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#000\">NT</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">~</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> normal</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">0</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> <br></span><span style=\"color:#660\">...</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#660\">}</span></div></code></div><p style=\"margin:0in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.0pt\"><br></p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin:0in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.0pt\">Cheers,</p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin:0in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.0pt\">Dominic </p></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "ZDxvJhNTlt4J",
   "from": "Ben Goodrich",
   "author": "743bbacc59f4b25f",
   "date": "2013-09-01T14:33:09Z",
   "subject": "Re: STAN syntax - fixing parameters",
   "parts": [
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">On Sunday, September 1, 2013 9:45:29 AM UTC-4, Dominic Edwards wrote: \r\n\r\n<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><p style=\"margin:0in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.0pt\">1: How can I fix one\r\nelement from a vector of parameter? </p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin:0in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.0pt\"> </p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"margin:0in;font-family:Calibri;font-size:11.0pt\">Initially I tried (attached as <i>ToyModel1.stan</i>) :</p>\r\n\r\n<div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">transformed parameters</span><span style=\"color:#660\">{</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#660\">...</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>vector</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">NT</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#606\">Beta</span><span style=\"color:#660\">;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> <br></span><span style=\"color:#606\">Beta</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">0</span><span style=\"color:#660\">;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> <br></span><span style=\"color:#660\">...</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#660\">}</span></div></code></div></div></blockquote></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><br>You want to declare the free elements in the parameters section and then copy them into a larger vector in the model section after specifying the fixed values. Then put the prior on the former. Like this<br><br><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px;word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">parameters </span><span style=\"color:#660\">{</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  vector</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">NT</span><span style=\"color:#660\">-</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#606\">Beta_free</span><span style=\"color:#660\">;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#660\">}</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>model </span><span style=\"color:#660\">{</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  vector</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">NT</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#606\">Beta</span><span style=\"color:#660\">;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  </span><span style=\"color:#606\">Beta</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">0.0</span><span style=\"color:#660\">;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  </span><span style=\"color:#008\">for</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">i </span><span style=\"color:#008\">in</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">NT</span><span style=\"color:#660\">-</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">))</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#606\">Beta</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">i</span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#606\">Beta_free</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">i</span><span style=\"color:#660\">];</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  </span><span style=\"color:#606\">Beta_free</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> normal</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">0.0</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">4.0</span><span style=\"color:#660\">);</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  </span><span style=\"color:#660\">...</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#660\">}</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span></div></code></div><br>In the next version of Stan, there is a tail() function which would allow you to write this without the explicit loop in the .stan file but either way it becomes a fast loop in the C++ code.<br><br>Ben<br><br></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "7veUVuFauloJ",
   "from": "Bob Carpenter",
   "author": "8aa9b8cc06fdc99c",
   "date": "2013-09-01T18:17:52Z",
   "subject": "Re: [stan-users] Re: STAN syntax - fixing parameters",
   "parts": [
    [
     2,
     "\r<br>\r<br>On 9/1/13 10:33 AM, Ben Goodrich wrote:\r<br>&gt; On Sunday, September 1, 2013 9:45:29 AM UTC-4, Dominic Edwards wrote:\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;     1: How can I fix one element from a vector of parameter?\r<br>&gt;\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "&gt;     Initially I tried (attached as /ToyModel1.stan/) :\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "&gt;\r<br>&gt;     |\r<br>&gt;     transformed parameters{\r<br>&gt;     ...\r<br>&gt;     vector[NT] Beta;\r<br>&gt;     Beta[1] &lt;- 0;\r<br>&gt;     ...\r<br>&gt;     }\r<br>&gt;     |\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; You want to declare the free elements in the parameters section and then copy them into a larger vector in the model\r<br>&gt; section after specifying the fixed values. Then put the prior on the former. Like this\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; |\r<br>&gt; parameters {\r<br>&gt;    vector[NT-1] Beta_free;\r<br>&gt; }\r<br>&gt; model {\r<br>&gt;    vector[NT] Beta;\r<br>&gt;    Beta[1] &lt;- 0.0;\r<br>&gt;    for (i in 1:(NT-1)) Beta[i+1] &lt;- Beta_free[i];\r<br>&gt;    Beta_free &lt;- normal(0.0, 4.0);\r<br>&gt;    ...\r<br>&gt; }\r<br>&gt; |\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; In the next version of Stan, there is a tail() function which would allow you to write this without the explicit loop in\r<br>&gt; the .stan file but either way it becomes a fast loop in the C++ code.\r<br>\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "I&#39;m afraid I don&#39;t have tail() [or head() or block()]\r<br>working as an lvalue (legal left side of assignment).\r<br>\r<br>We could (a) write special block-assignment functions (easy), (b) figure\r<br>out how to do a better job with lvalues on the LHS (more challenging, at\r<br>least for me), or (c) cobble something together\r<br>like I did for rows of matrices using indexing (that is, if a is a matrix,\r<br>then a[n] is a row that&#39;s a valid lvalue).\r<br>\r<br>I&#39;d probably opt for (c) if it&#39;s an immediate need.\r<br>\r<br>- Bob\r<br>"
    ]
   ]
  }
 ]
}
